  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I found no gap in the `habeas:coverage` citation on `src/execution_coverage/progress.rs#mod[tests]/impl[RecordingTerm/indicatif::TermLike]/fn[move_cursor_left]`. One claim in its commentary is loosely worded (see Residual doubt).\n\n- **Form:** The citation is a fenced `{cite}` with commentary on an explicit function with a body. That is the form `habeas:coverage` allows for a function-level exception. The item carries no `{impl}` or `{test}` reference to the same target, so it is not redundant.\n- **Substance:** The function is a `Ok(())` no-op on `RecordingTerm`. That type sits inside `#[cfg(test)] mod tests`, so only unit tests compile it. `TermLike` requires the callback, and the application's own code never reaches this method. The commentary says the fixed-width display tests request no horizontal cursor movement. The source agrees: `stage_and_conversion_counts_are_visible_then_cleared`, `preparation_activity_keeps_updating_without_a_stage_change` and `terminal_policy_and_hidden_progress_emit_no_display` only draw, record and clear. Nothing calls `move_cursor_left`. Being unexecuted follows from the adapter being test-only and from `TermLike` requiring the method. That is a substantive reason, not just difficulty.\n- **Reliance on the PTY test:** `tests/execution_coverage.rs#fn[coverage_progress_on_a_pty_preserves_reports_and_plain_output_policy]` exists. It runs the real terminal path in a PTY and asserts in-place escape output (`\\x1b[`) and line clearing (`\\x1b[2K`), plus plain output for dumb or non-terminal runs. That supports the claim that real terminal behavior is covered separately from this unit-test adapter.\n- **Residual doubt:** The PTY test does not specifically assert horizontal-left cursor movement. The commentary's phrase \"real terminal movement\" is therefore loose. It does not undercut the exception, because the adapter has no application behavior to test.",
